        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Care priority occupations</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">31</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">of them are health care NOC codes</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-amber-600 mb-2">76</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Lowest Care: Health cutoff in 2026 (Sep 10 and Oct 8)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        On April 23, 2026 the BC Provincial Nominee Program reorganized its priorities around three objectives: Care, Build and Innovate. Care is the largest occupation list of the three. WelcomeBC says it covers "36 in-demand occupations in healthcare, education, childcare and veterinary care." If you work in one of these occupations in B.C., or have a job offer in one, you are no longer competing against every skilled worker in the pool. You compete only against others in your Care group. In 2026 that meant invitations at scores far below the general points cutoff. This guide lists every code and explains the requirements that can make or break an application.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-amber-900 mb-2">2027 status</p>
        <p class="text-amber-800">The list below is the one in force on October 14, 2026, taken from WelcomeBC's selection of workers page. The BC PNP has not announced its 2027 priorities. We expect the Care framework to carry into 2027 because it is built into the current program guide, but that is our analysis, not an announcement.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How the 36 occupations break down</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Health care:</strong> 31 NOC codes, including NOC 33102 health care assistants, who must be on the BC Care Aide &amp; Community Health Worker Registry</li>
        <li><strong>Childcare:</strong> 1 code, NOC 42202 early childhood educators, who need an ECE One Year or Five Year certificate</li>
        <li><strong>Education:</strong> 2 codes, NOC 41220 and 41221, French-speaking teachers only</li>
        <li><strong>Veterinary care:</strong> 2 codes, NOC 31103 and 32104</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        In the draws, these became four Care draw types: <em>Care: Health</em>, <em>Care: Childcare</em>, <em>Care: Education</em> and <em>Care: Veterinary Care</em>. Each is run separately with its own cutoff.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">The health care list (31 NOC codes) by group</h2>

      <p>
        The codes and titles below match WelcomeBC's list. The "licensing to check" column names the B.C. body we confirmed for each group. Where we could not confirm a single provincial regulator, we say so. Do not assume you can skip licensing. Section 3.7 of the program guide requires you to be qualified for the job, including any mandatory licence, certification or registration.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Group 1: Physicians and health care management</h3>
