      <p class="text-base text-slate-600">
        Note on regulators: B.C. merged several health colleges on June 28, 2024. Dietitians, occupational therapists, physical therapists, psychologists and speech and hearing professionals moved into the College of Health and Care Professionals of BC. Chiropractors and traditional Chinese medicine practitioners moved into the College of Complementary Health Professionals of BC. Older documents, including some BC PNP materials, may still use the former college names.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Childcare, education and veterinary care</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">NOC</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Occupation</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Condition for a targeted invitation</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">42202</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Early childhood educators</td><td class="px-4 py-3">ECE One Year or ECE Five Year certificate from the ECE Registry (an ECE Assistant certificate does not qualify)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">41220</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Secondary school teachers (French-speaking only)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Employed in B.C.'s public K-12 system and CLB 5 or higher in French</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">41221</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Elementary/Kindergarten teachers (French-speaking only)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Same as 41220</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">31103</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Veterinarians</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Registration with the College of Veterinarians of BC (CVBC) applies to practice</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3">32104</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Animal health technologists and veterinary technicians</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The draws targeted those "with valid professional designation" (the program guide refers to registration with the BC Veterinary Technologists Association)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        For ECEs, the certificate type is the whole game. According to the B.C. government's ECE pages, the <strong>Five Year</strong> certificate needs a recognized basic ECE program (or equivalent) plus 500 hours of supervised work experience. The <strong>One Year</strong> certificate lets you work as a fully certified ECE while you complete those 500 hours. It must be renewed within a year if the hours are not done, and it can be renewed only once. The <strong>Assistant</strong> certificate needs only one recent course and does not qualify for the Care draw or for BC PNP professional-designation points. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-early-childhood-educators-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">ECE 2027 guide</a> goes into detail. French-speaking teachers should read our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-french-speaking-teachers-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">French teachers guide</a>, and veterinary professionals our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-veterinarians-vet-technologists-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">vets and vet technologists guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Two routes in: Health Authority stream vs Care draws</h2>

      <p>
        Health workers have two different BC PNP routes, and many people mix them up. They have different occupation lists, different employers and a different process.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Feature</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Health Authority stream</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Care: Health targeted draw</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Employer</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A B.C. public health authority (Fraser, Interior, Island, Northern, Vancouver Coastal, Provincial Health Services, First Nations Health Authority, Providence Health Care)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Any eligible B.C. employer, such as a private clinic, pharmacy, care home or dental office</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Registration and invitation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Not needed; you apply directly</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Register in the Skills Immigration pool and wait for an invitation</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A longer list of 46 codes, including some not on the Care list, such as 32124 pharmacy technicians, 32201 massage therapists, 33100 licensed dental assistants and 42201 social and community service workers</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The 31 Care health codes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Employer support</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The health authority must agree to support you, and only certain staff can sign the Employer Declaration Form</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Your employer must meet the Part 6 employer requirements</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Physicians, NPs, midwives</td><td class="px-4 py-3">May use it without being direct health-authority employees. Physicians and NPs must be licensed or eligible for licensure and supported by a health authority; midwives must be licensed and supported by a health authority or midwife practice group</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Eligible if registered with an eligible employer</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        If you work directly for a health authority in an eligible occupation, the Health Authority stream is usually the simpler route, because there is no score race. The catch is that health authorities are not obliged to support applications, and each one has its own internal process. Ask your HR department first. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-health-authority-stream" class="text-blue-600 underline">Health Authority stream guide</a> covers the steps.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How a Care draw actually works</h2>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>You register</strong> (free) in the Skills Immigration pool with your job offer's NOC code, wage, location, education, experience and language scores. You can have only one active BC PNP registration or application at a time.</li>
        <li><strong>The program filters</strong> the pool to registrants whose NOC is on the relevant Care list and who meet any condition (Registry, ECE certificate, French CLB 5 and a public K-12 job, or a veterinary designation).</li>
        <li><strong>It ranks by score</strong> and invites from the top down to that round's limit. The lowest score invited becomes the published "minimum score".</li>
        <li><strong>You have 30 calendar days</strong> to submit a complete application and pay the $1,750 application fee. The 30 days cannot be extended.</li>
        <li><strong>Your application is checked against the targeting factor.</strong> Section 7.3 of the program guide says that if you did not, or no longer, meet the factors used to target you, the application will be refused. If your registration listed NOC 33102 and you are not actually on the Registry, you lose the application.</li>
        <li><strong>Unused registrations expire</strong> after 12 months in the pool.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">2026 Care draw results</h3>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Draw</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">May 6</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Jun 2</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Jul 9</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Aug 6</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Sep 10</th><th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-slate-700">Oct 8</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Health</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">96</td><td class="px-4 py-3">84</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Childcare</td><td class="px-4 py-3">115</td><td class="px-4 py-3">111</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td><td class="px-4 py-3">102</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td><td class="px-4 py-3">99</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Veterinary</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">92</td><td class="px-4 py-3">88</td><td class="px-4 py-3">72</td><td class="px-4 py-3">90</td><td class="px-4 py-3">86</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Education</td><td class="px-4 py-3">–</td><td class="px-4 py-3">–</td><td class="px-4 py-3">–</td><td class="px-4 py-3">68</td><td class="px-4 py-3">–</td><td class="px-4 py-3">–</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Care: Health issued 699 invitations across these six rounds and Care: Childcare 719. For comparison, the points track of the Innovate draw on September 24, 2026 needed 131. The full history is in our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2026-draws-recap-2027-forecast" class="text-blue-600 underline">2026 draws recap</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Five points many Care workers forget to claim</h2>

      <p>
        Section 8.2 of the program guide awards <strong>5 extra points</strong> for an eligible B.C. professional designation linked to your job offer. For Care occupations, the eligible designations include:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Health care aides (NOC 33102) registered with the BC Care Aide &amp; Community Health Worker Registry</li>
        <li>Practical nurses (NOC 32101) licensed with BCCNM</li>
        <li>ECEs (NOC 42202) with a valid One Year or Five Year certificate (Assistant certificates are excluded)</li>
        <li>Dental hygienists (NOC 32111) and dental technicians (NOC 32112) registered with the BC College of Oral Health Professionals</li>
        <li>Traditional Chinese medicine practitioners and acupuncturists (NOC 32200) licensed in B.C.</li>
        <li>Veterinary technicians (NOC 32104) registered with the BC Veterinary Technologists Association</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        In draws where cutoffs moved 4 to 8 points between rounds, those 5 points can decide the outcome. Run your numbers in the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Getting licensed: the three most common routes</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Internationally educated nurses (RN, RPN, LPN)</h3>
      <p>
        BCCNM tells internationally educated RNs to apply first to <strong>Inspire Global Assessments</strong>, which checks identity, English proficiency and education and runs a competency assessment. BCCNM recommends opening your BCCNM application at the same time. After any required coursework you write the NCLEX-RN. BCCNM says most fees can be reimbursed through the Internationally Educated Nurse Bursary Program. It also warns that applications must be completed by the applicant personally, and that agencies offering to file them for a fee are not endorsed. Details are in our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-nurses-health-care-assistants-2027" class="text-blue-600 underline">nurses and HCAs guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Health care assistants trained abroad</h3>
      <p>
        The Care Aide Registry has a separate category for internationally educated applicants. You upload proof of training, English results if English is not your first language, and a credential evaluation (the Registry lists ICES and equivalents such as NNAS, WES and IQAS). The Registry may then refer you to an HCA Competency Assessment run by Inspire. If your performance report shows no undemonstrated competencies, you are registered directly. Otherwise you may need remedial education first. The Registry says HCAs must be registered to work in a public health care setting in B.C.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Internationally trained physicians</h3>
      <p>
        CPSBC offers several licence classes for physicians with a medical degree from outside Canada, including provisional, assessment and associate physician classes. Family physicians often enter through PRA-BC, a clinical field assessment followed by a return-of-service commitment in a B.C. community. Physicians supported by a health authority can use the Health Authority stream even if they are not health-authority employees. Our <a href="/articles/express-entry-foreign-medical-doctors-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 underline">foreign medical doctors guide</a> covers the federal Express Entry side.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Worked examples</h2>

      <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
        <p class="font-semibold mb-2">Example 1: LPN at a private care home in Kamloops</p>
        <p class="text-base">NOC 32101 is on the Care health list, and BCCNM licensure is in place. Kamloops is outside Metro Vancouver and the five Area 2 municipalities, so the job earns 15 area points. The LPN licence adds 5 professional-designation points. This person registers in the Skills Immigration pool and competes in Care: Health draws, where 2026 cutoffs ranged from 108 to 76.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
        <p class="font-semibold mb-2">Example 2: Unregistered care aide in Surrey</p>
        <p class="text-base">The job is classified under NOC 33102, but the worker is not yet on the Registry. They cannot receive a targeted Care invitation for 33102, and section 3.7 of the guide makes Registry listing a qualification requirement for this NOC anyway. Step one is Registry registration, not a BC PNP registration.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
        <p class="font-semibold mb-2">Example 3: Pharmacy technician at a hospital run by Island Health</p>
        <p class="text-base">NOC 32124 is <em>not</em> on the 31-code Care list, so there are no Care: Health draws for this occupation. It <em>is</em> on the Health Authority stream list. With Island Health's support, this person can apply directly without registering or waiting for a draw.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Who this list is for, and who it is not for</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Good fit:</strong> licensed or registered workers in the 36 occupations with a full-time, indeterminate B.C. job offer (some teaching roles may use limited-term offers under Appendix A of the guide), especially outside Metro Vancouver.</li>
        <li><strong>Needs work first:</strong> people in a Care occupation who still lack the required registration, certificate or licence.</li>
        <li><strong>Not covered:</strong> health-adjacent jobs outside the list, such as 33100 dental assistants or 32201 massage therapists with a private employer. Without a health-authority job offer, these workers had only the Innovate draws in 2026, where the points cutoff stayed above 130. Medical administrative assistants (13112) are now on the guide's list of ineligible occupations for every stream.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2">
        <li>Choosing a NOC code to fit a draw rather than the actual duties. The NOC and work location you register with can be used as selection factors, and a mismatch at application can lead to refusal.</li>
        <li>Registering as an ECE with only an Assistant certificate.</li>
        <li>Assuming a non-health-authority employer can use the Health Authority stream.</li>
        <li>Letting a language test expire. Results are valid for two years and must be valid at registration to earn points.</li>
        <li>Paying agencies to submit licensing applications. BCCNM specifically warns against this, and BC PNP rules require any paid representative to be a licensed consultant or lawyer in good standing.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What might change for 2027 (analysis)</h2>
      <p>
        Three things are worth watching. First, BC's 2027 allocation: it has not been announced, and it decides how many Care rounds the program can run. Second, the list itself. The April 2026 update said the ineligible occupations and employers list would expand, and the May 28, 2026 program guide added a list of ineligible occupations, so changes to priority lists are a normal tool. Third, the federal side. Health care and social services was one of IRCC's 2026 Express Entry categories, and IRCC consulted from August 4 to September 1, 2026 on 2027 categories, including the option of narrowing labour-shortage categories. If the federal health category shrinks, the BC PNP becomes more important for health workers, not less. See our <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 underline">Express Entry 2027 guide</a> and the <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-2027-outlook-predictions" class="text-blue-600 underline">BC PNP 2027 outlook</a>.
      </p>

